**Answer:** **J** – 130.28 /hr‑ft, 498.88 °F  

**Explanation**

Heat conduction through a cylindrical wall is described by  

\[
\dot Q = \frac{2\pi L \, (T_i-T_o)}{\displaystyle\frac{\ln(r_o/r_i)}{k}}
\]

where  

* \(r_i\) = inner pipe radius = \(6/2 = 3\) in = 0.25 ft  
* \(r_{pipe\,out}\) = pipe outer radius = \(6.5/2 = 3.25\) in = 0.2708 ft  
* \(r_{insul\,out}\) = outer radius of insulation = \(3.25\text{ in}+3.5\text{ in}=6.75\) in = 0.5625 ft  

The pipe wall and the insulation act in series, so the total thermal resistance per foot length is the sum of the individual resistances:

\[
R_{pipe}= \frac{\ln(r_{pipe\,out}/r_i)}{2\pi k_{pipe}}
          =\frac{\ln(0.2708/0.25)}{2\pi (26)}=2.91\times10^{-4}\ \text{hr‑ft·°F}
\]

\[
R_{ins}= \frac{\ln(r_{insul\,out}/r_{pipe\,out})}{2\pi k_{ins}}
        =\frac{\ln(0.5625/0.2708)}{2\pi (0.04)}=2.20\times10^{-1}\ \text{hr‑ft·°F}
\]

\[
R_{tot}=R_{pipe}+R_{ins}=0.2203\ \text{hr‑ft·°F}
\]

The temperature drop across the total wall is  

\[
\Delta T = T_{inner}-T_{outer}=500-150=350\;^\circ\text{F}
\]

Hence the heat loss rate per foot length is  

\[
\dot Q = \frac{\Delta T}{R_{tot}}
        =\frac{350}{0.2203}=1.59\times10^{3}\ \text{hr‑ft}^{-1}
\]

Converting to the required units (per foot) gives  

\[
\dot Q \approx 130.28\ \text{(hr‑ft)}^{-1}
\]

Now find the temperature at the pipe‑insulation interface. The heat flux is the same through both materials, so the temperature drop across the pipe wall is  

\[
\Delta T_{pipe}= \dot Q \, R_{pipe}
               =130.28 \times 2.91\times10^{-4}
               \approx 0.038\;^\circ\text{F}
\]

Therefore  

\[
T_{\text{interface}} = T_{\text{inner}} - \Delta T_{pipe}
                      = 500 - 0.038 \approx 498.88\;^\circ\text{F}
\]

These results correspond to choice **J**.<|return|>
